<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marsilius of Padua</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1275–c. 1342</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marsilius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> was an Italian political thinker whose treatise Defensor pacis (1324) argued that political authority comes from the people and that the Church should hold no coercive power. Condemned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a>, he fled to the court of Louis IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, accompanied him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1328 and remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> until his death around 1342. His work is a landmark of medieval political theory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marsilius-of-padua.jpg" alt="Marsilius of Padua" width="320" height="289" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marsilio_da_Padova.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marsilius was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, around 1275, with the family name Mainardi or Mainardini. He was trained in medicine, probably at the University of Padua, and practised a variety of professions. He later went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a>, where he became a devoted admirer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, whom he called the divine philosopher. He served as rector of the University of Paris approximately in 1312 and 1313, and probably taught there before and after his term.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Marsilius wrote Defensor pacis in 1324, during a power struggle between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a> and Louis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, the elected candidate for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>. In 1323 Louis had sent an army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> against the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, a strong ally of the pope; John excommunicated him and demanded that he give up his claim to the imperial crown. Marsilius argued from reason and from authority that the empire was independent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and that the powers claimed by the popes were empty. Much of the work is theology: relying on Scripture, he sought to show that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> claimed no temporal power and that the church should be subordinate to the state in both secular and spiritual matters.</p><p>Its political philosophy is what it is remembered for. Marsilius agreed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> that government serves the natural human desire for a sufficient life, but went beyond him in embracing a republicanism in which the people are the only legitimate source of authority. The people should elect, correct and if necessary depose their rulers, and democracy, he argued, tends to produce the wisest laws. He held that scripture stands above the church and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> was created by human arrangement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a> declared a number of his views heretical in 1327. In the later Defensor minor he took up ecclesiastical jurisdiction, indulgences, excommunication and marriage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Marsilius and John of Jandun, sometimes credited as a co-author, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for Louis's court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, where Louis protected them together with Michael of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesena">Cesena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-ockham">William of Ockham</a>. In 1326 Marsilius went with Louis to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Louis was crowned King of Italy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1327 and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in January 1328, where he was crowned emperor and declared John XXII deposed for heresy. Marsilius was appointed archbishop of Milan as recompense for his services. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> around 1342, still unreconciled to the Church. Some scholars see Defensor pacis as the most revolutionary political treatise of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and he is sometimes seen as a forerunner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marsilius of Padua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Louis IV, the first Wittelsbach emperor, opposed the papacy and sheltered critics such as William of Ockham and <b>Marsilius of Padua</b> at his court in Munich.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Significant contributions to the principles of modern secularism came from theologians and Christian writers such as St. Augustine, William of Ockham, <b>Marsilius of Padua</b>, Martin Luther, Roger Williams, John Locke and Talleyrand.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/secularism">Secularism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.41,11.87&amp;year=1318" aria-label="Padua on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4832 562 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Marsilius of Padua"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5282 832h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5282 832h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.41,11.87&amp;year=1318">What was here in 1318? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
